                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Correct. The rate law can be experimentally determined by changing the initial concentration of each reactant and observing the change in reaction rate. If the concentration of a reactant is doubled and the observed reaction rate also doubles, then the order of the reaction (the exponent) for that reactant must be 1: doubling the concentration means doubling the rate, 2"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "1"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " = 2. If the concentration of a reactant is increased by a factor of three and the observed reaction rate increases by nine, then the order of the reaction (the exponent) for that reactant must be 2, since 3"
